LAN Topology Design:
	1) 3 Layer Design:
		> Efficient for handling North-South traffic <= heavier bottom-layer to top-layer traffic <= heavier internal<->external (end-user<->router) traffic
		> Network topoplogy should be designed for scalability, performance, & security
		> Each layer has it own design priciples and characteristics
		> Network topology split in 3 layers:
			1) Access Layer:
				> Bottom layer:
					> Connects end-hosts
					> Bottom layer for each building
					> Simple switches => Highport count but low bandwidth support
				> Purpose: High port count at affordable price <= connects end-hosts
				> Redundancy: No redundant connection to end-hosts since mosts only have 1 nic anyway
				> Additional feature: Client access security measures are enabled at access layer
			2) Distribution Layer:
				> Middle layer:
					> Each access layer uplinks to a pair of dirtibution switches
					> Upgraded switches => Higher bandwidth support but less port count => Heavier support for traffic flowing b/w router & end-users
					> Top layer for each building
				> Purpose: Aggragation point for access layer, provides scalability
				> Redundancy: Deployed in redundant pairs
				> Additional feature: Most software policies such as QOS enabled at distribution layer
			3) Core Layer:
				> Top layer:
					> Each distribution layer uplinks to a pair core switches
					> Upgraded switches => Higher bandwidth support but less port count => Heavier support for traffic flowing b/w router & end-users
					> Top layer for entire network topology | Connects the top layer of each building into a single top layer
					> Core switch pair uplinks to a WAN edge router pair
				> Purpose: Designed for speed and resilliency
				> Redundancy: Deployed in redundant pairs
				> Addition feature: NO software policies enabled <= software policies slow down a switch
		> Smaller LAN topology uses collapsed core+distribution layer => distribution & core layer functions performed on same switch
	2) Spine-Leaf Data Center Design:
		> Efficient for handling East-West traffic: <= heavier bottom-layer to bottom-layer traffic <= heavier internal<->internal (end-user<->end-user) traffic
		> eg. app split across multiple servers
		> LAN topology split into 2 tiers:
			1) Tier1/Leaf:
				> Bottom layer => Connects end-users => Similar to Access-layer
			2) Tier2/Spine:
				> Top layer => Aggregates the bottom layer => Allows traffic flow b/w end-users => Similar to Core-Distribution-layer
				> Instead of single switch pair, multiple spine switches are put for increases end-user bandwidth
		> Difference:
			> Spine-Leaf Design: Structured for horizaontal scalability => Higher end-user bandwidth => Supports heavier end-user<->end-user traffic
			> 3 layer Design: Scructured for vertical scalability => Higher uplinking bandwidth => Supports heavier router<->end-user traffic

VLANS:
	> Router Operations: Splits network into separate domains at layer 3 => Improved performace and security
		> Host in different IP subnets must send traffic via router to communicate
		> Security rules can be applied on router/firewall to allow only selected traffic b/w different IP subnets
		> Routers do not forward broadcast traffic
	> Switch Operations: Do NOT split network into separate domains at layer 2 => No performance and security
		> No security control on traffic b/w hosts
		> Switches DO forward broadcast traffic by default (even across different IP subnets)
	> VLANs (Virtual Local Area Networks):
		> Segment the LAN into separate broadcast domains at layer 2 => Increased perfomance and security
		> Typically 1-to-1 relationship b/w an IP subnet & a VLAN => A router is required to be connected to the switch for inter-VLAN communication
		> All switch interfaces are by default configured in VLAN 1 (default) => Single broadcast domain
	> Types of switch-ports:
		1) Access Ports:
			> Configured on interfaces where end-hosts are plugged in
			> Access ports are configured with 1 specific vlan
			> Access ports handle traffic for only that vlan with which they are configured
			> Port configuration is all on the switch, end-hosts are not vlan aware
		2) Trunk Ports:
			> Inter-switch linking interfaces are configured as trunk ports
			> Handle traffic for multiple/all allowed vlans
			> Allows for connectivity within a vlan across multiple switches
			> Dot1Q Tagging through Dot1Q/802.1Q trunking protocol:
				1) When a frame leaves a trunk port, a tag specifying the vlan no the frame belongs to, is added to the frame header
				2) On the recieving switch, the dot1q tag is removed from the frame header, and the frame is marked for the specified vlan
				3) Frame is forwarded out access ports belonging to the specified vlan (without dot1q tag), & all trunk ports (with dot1q tag reattached)
			> Native Vlan:
				> Any untagged traffic recieved on a trunk port is put in the native vlan
				> Default native vlan: Vlan 1
				> For security native vlan is recommended be changed to an unused vlan
				> Native vlans must match on both sides of the trunking link for trunk ports to come up
			> Allowed Vlans:
				> An allowed vlans list can be configured on a trunk port => Limits traffic passing thru the trunk port to only specified vlans => Better security
				> Traffic for any other vlan would not be allowed to pass through the trunk port
			> DTP (Dynamic Trunking Protocol):
				> If 2 cisco switches are linked, they can automatically negotiate a trunk connection using cisco's DTP
				> Manual config is recommended over using DTP for configuring a trunk connection
				> DTP Config:
					1) auto
						> cmd: switchport mode dynamic auto
						> forms trunk if port on other side is set to trunk or desirable
						> trunk is not fromed if both sides set to auto
						> default on newer switches
					2) desirable:
						> cmd: switchport mode dynamic desirable
						> will from trunk is opposite port is set to trunk, desirable, or auto
						> default on older switches
					3) no negotiate:
						> cmd: switchport nonegotiate
						> disables DTP
			> VTP (VLAN Trunking Protocol):
				> Allows to synchronize the VLAN database across switches in the same VTP domain
				> Allows to add/edit/delete VLANs on switches configured as VTP Servers and have switches configured as VTP Clients automatically sync their VLAN db
				> VTP domain name must match on neighbour switches for trunks to be negotiated by DTP
				> VTP Modes:
					1) VTP Server:
						> Can add/edit/delete VLANs
						> Synchronizes it VLAN db from another VTP server with a higher revision number
					2) VTP Client:
						> Cannot add/edit/delete VLANs
						> Synchronizes it VLAN db from the VTP server with the highest revision number
					3) VTP Transparent: 
						> Does not participate in the VTP Domain
						> Does not advertise or learn VLAN info but passes it on
						> Can add/edit/delete VLANs in its local VLAN database


